282.3 130.4L283.1 131.8L283.9 133.1L284.7 134.4L285.5 135.4L286.3 136.1L287.1 136.6L287.9 136.9L288.8 137.1L289.6 137.2L290.4 137.2L291.2 136.9L292 137L292.8 137.3L293.6 137.6L294.4 137.8L295.2 137.8L296 138" fill="none" stroke="#c4783a" stroke-width="3" stroke-linejoin="round"/><circle cx="104.3" cy="159.9" r="4.5" fill="#c4783a" stroke="#fff" stroke-width="1.5"/><text class="cl" x="104.3" y="181.9" text-anchor="middle" fill="#8a5226">2006 high</text><circle cx="154.3" cy="213.6" r="4.5" fill="#c4783a" stroke="#fff" stroke-width="1.5"/><text class="cl" x="154.3" y="201.6" text-anchor="middle" fill="#8a5226">2012 low</text><circle cx="267.8" cy="118.7" r="4.5" fill="#c4783a" stroke="#fff" stroke-width="1.5"/><text class="cl" x="261.8" y="106.7" text-anchor="start" fill="#8a5226">2023 high</text><text class="cl" x="302" y="57.9" fill="#2f6f8f">$364k</text><text class="cl" x="302" y="143" fill="#8a5226">$231k</text></svg><figcaption>Zillow's typical value of a home in the middle third of prices, each month from November 2001. Houses means single-family homes.</figcaption></figure><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Condos took about 15 years to pass their 2006 high again, and houses about 13. Today the typical condo is worth more than a tenth less than at its 2023 high. The typical house is within 2 percent of its own high.</p><div style="overflow-x:auto;margin:1.2rem 0;max-width:760px"><table aria-label="Typical condo and house value in the Myrtle Beach metro" style="width:100%;border-collapse:collapse;font-size:.92rem"><thead><tr><th style="padding:.55rem .8rem;border-bottom:2px solid var(--navy);color:var(--navy);text-align:left;font-family:var(--sans);font-size:.8rem;letter-spacing:.04em;text-transform:uppercase">When</th><th style="padding:.55rem .8rem;border-bottom:2px solid var(--navy);color:var(--navy);text-align:left;font-family:var(--sans);font-size:.8rem;letter-spacing:.04em;text-transform:uppercase">Typical condo</th><th style="padding:.55rem .8rem;border-bottom:2px solid var(--navy);color:var(--navy);text-align:left;font-family:var(--sans);font-size:.8rem;letter-spacing:.04em;text-transform:uppercase">Typical house</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td style="padding:.55rem .8rem;border-bottom:1px solid var(--rule);color:var(--muted)">Late 2001</td><td style="padding:.55rem .8rem;border-bottom:1px solid var(--rule);color:var(--muted)">$112,000</td><td style="padding:.55rem .8rem;border-bottom:1px solid var(--rule);color:var(--muted)">$146,000</td></tr><tr><td style="padding:.55rem .8rem;border-bottom:1px solid var(--rule);color:var(--muted)">High, 2006 or 2007</td><td style="padding:.55rem .8rem;border-bottom:1px solid var(--rule);color:var(--muted)">$197,000</td><td style="padding:.55rem .8rem;border-bottom:1px solid var(--rule);color:var(--muted)">$243,000</td></tr><tr><td style="padding:.55rem .8rem;border-bottom:1px solid var(--rule);color:var(--muted)">Low, 2011 or 2012</td><td style="padding:.55rem .8rem;border-bottom:1px solid var(--rule);color:var(--muted)">$113,000</td><td style="padding:.55rem .8rem;border-bottom:1px solid var(--rule);color:var(--muted)">$169,000</td></tr><tr><td style="padding:.55rem .8rem;border-bottom:1px solid var(--rule);color:var(--muted)">Latest month</td><td style="padding:.55rem .8rem;border-bottom:1px solid var(--rule);color:var(--muted)">$231,000</td><td style="padding:.55rem .8rem;border-bottom:1px solid var(--rule);color:var(--muted)">$364,000</td></tr><tr><td style="padding:.55rem .8rem;border-bottom:1px solid var(--rule);color:var(--muted)">Since 2001</td><td style="padding:.55rem .8rem;border-bottom:1px solid var(--rule);color:var(--muted)">Up 107%</td><td style="padding:.55rem .8rem;border-bottom:1px solid var(--rule);color:var(--muted)">Up 149%</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">The typical metro condo is now worth about 64 percent of the typical house, about as low as it has been in 25 years. In mid-2006 it was about 83 percent.</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">For an investor, a condo here is cheap next to a house today. Its value has not grown steadily, so buy it for its income and its lower price.</p></div></section><section style="background:var(--ivory-2)" id="prices-to-buy"><div class="wrap"><h2 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.7rem;color:var(--navy);margin-bottom:1rem;line-height:1.3" data-toc="Prices to buy">What do condos cost to buy in Myrtle Beach and North Myrtle Beach?</h2><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">About $224,000 was the middle price of condos sold in Horry County in the past year.</p><figure class="c3-gchart"><div role="table" aria-label="Middle condo sale price in Horry County, by area"><span class="cap" aria-hidden="true">Middle condo sale price in Horry County, by area</span><div role="rowgroup" class="sr"><div role="row"><span role="columnheader">Area</span><span role="columnheader">middle price</span></div></div><div role="rowgroup"><div role="row"><span role="rowheader"><span class="n">North Myrtle Beach</span></span><span role="cell"><span class="b" style="--f:1" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="v">$340,000</span></span></div><div role="row"><span role="rowheader"><span class="n">Outside city limits and other towns</span></span><span role="cell"><span class="b" style="--f:0.588" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="v">$200,000</span></span></div><div role="row"><span role="rowheader"><span class="n">City of Myrtle Beach</span></span><span role="cell"><span class="b" style="--f:0.514" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="v">$175,000</span></span></div><div role="row" class="ref"><span role="rowheader"><span class="n">Every other kind of home, for comparison</span></span><span role="cell"><span class="b" style="--f:0.959" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="v">$326,000</span></span></div></div></div><figcaption>Deeds Horry County recorded in the past year. The areas follow city limits. Every other kind of home means houses, townhouses outside a condo and mobile homes on owned land.</figcaption></figure><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">North Myrtle Beach condos sell for about twice the City of Myrtle Beach's. Asking prices in oceanfront buildings show the same split.</p><h3 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.25rem;color:var(--navy);margin:1.6rem 0 .6rem">Where are the condos?</h3><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">The county has about 41,000 condo units. About 12,000 are in North Myrtle Beach, about 12,000 in the City of Myrtle Beach, and about 17,000 outside city limits and in the smaller towns. On the ocean, towers and low-rise buildings stand side by side:</p><div class="c3ph-gal" role="group" aria-label="Oceanfront condo buildings in North Myrtle Beach, the City of Myrtle Beach and Garden City Beach" style="--c3ph-n:3;--c3ph-r:16/9"><figure><div class="c3ph-f"><img src="/images/2026-11-a/invest-condos/north-myrtle-beach-condos-2011-1200w.webp" srcset="/images/2026-11-a/invest-condos/north-myrtle-beach-condos-2011-600w.webp 600w, /images/2026-11-a/invest-condos/north-myrtle-beach-condos-2011-1200w.webp 1200w" sizes="(max-width: 699px) 100vw, 360px" width="1200" height="675" alt="A wide white condo tower and three smaller condo buildings on the dunes in North Myrtle Beach, with a road and beach houses behind them and the beach in front" loading="lazy" decoding="async"><span class="c3ph-cr"><a href="https://www.usa.gov/publicdomain/label/1.0/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" aria-label="Photo: USGS. License: Public domain" title="License: Public domain">Photo: USGS</a></span></div><figcaption>North Myrtle Beach: a condo tower and low-rise condo buildings on South Ocean Boulevard, with beach houses behind, seen from a plane in 2011.</figcaption></figure><figure><div class="c3ph-f"><img src="/images/2026-11-a/invest-condos/myrtle-beach-condo-towers-2011-1200w.webp" srcset="/images/2026-11-a/invest-condos/myrtle-beach-condo-towers-2011-600w.webp 600w, /images/2026-11-a/invest-condos/myrtle-beach-condo-towers-2011-1200w.webp 1200w" sizes="(max-width: 699px) 100vw, 360px" width="1200" height="675" alt="Three tall oceanfront condo towers behind the dunes in Myrtle Beach, with smaller condo buildings across a curving road to the right and the beach and ocean in front" loading="lazy" decoding="async"><span class="c3ph-cr"><a href="https://www.usa.gov/publicdomain/label/1.0/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" aria-label="Photo: USGS. License: Public domain" title="License: Public domain">Photo: USGS</a></span></div><figcaption>City of Myrtle Beach: condo towers on North Ocean Boulevard, seen from a plane in 2011.</figcaption></figure><figure><div class="c3ph-f"><img src="/images/2026-11-a/invest-condos/garden-city-condos-2011-1200w.webp" srcset="/images/2026-11-a/invest-condos/garden-city-condos-2011-600w.webp 600w, /images/2026-11-a/invest-condos/garden-city-condos-2011-1200w.webp 1200w" sizes="(max-width: 699px) 100vw, 360px" width="1200" height="675" alt="A tall tan condo tower and several low-rise condo buildings with pools on the beach at Garden City, with beach houses and a wide salt marsh behind them" loading="lazy" decoding="async"><span class="c3ph-cr"><a href="https://www.usa.gov/publicdomain/label/1.0/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" aria-label="Photo: USGS. License: Public domain" title="License: Public domain">Photo: USGS</a></span></div><figcaption>Garden City Beach, outside city limits: a condo tower and low-rise condo buildings on North Waccamaw Drive, with the marsh behind, seen from a plane in 2011.</figcaption></figure></div><h3 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.25rem;color:var(--navy);margin:1.6rem 0 .6rem">How cheap can a Myrtle Beach condo be?</h3><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">About 4 in 10 condos sold for under $200,000, against about 1 in 7 other homes.</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Oceanfront units still start under $200,000: about half of the condos for sale in oceanfront buildings ask less. Most are small: about 6 in 10 are studios or one-bedrooms.</p><div style="border-left:3px solid var(--brass);background:var(--ivory);padding:1.2rem 1.4rem;margin:1.8rem 0;max-width:720px"><p style="color:var(--navy);font-weight:600;margin-bottom:.4rem">Get a list of condos for sale that fit you</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.7;margin-bottom:.8rem">Tell us your budget and the town. We send you the condos for sale in that price range.</p><a class="btn btn-brass" href="/contact/">Have us send condos that fit</a></div></div></section><section style="background:var(--ivory)" id="yearly-cost"><div class="wrap"><h2 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.7rem;color:var(--navy);margin-bottom:1rem;line-height:1.3" data-toc="Yearly cost">How much does it cost to own a Myrtle Beach condo each year?</h2><div class="c3-cd-side"><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">About $18,000 a year before insurance and the loan, for the middle two-bedroom for sale in an oceanfront building with a Myrtle Beach address. The HOA fee is most of it.</p><figure class="c3-cd-fig2"><div class="c3-cd-bill" role="table" aria-label="What one typical oceanfront condo costs to own for a year, before insurance and the loan"><p class="hd" aria-hidden="true">One typical oceanfront condo, a year</p><div role="row"><span role="rowheader">HOA fee <small>$1,075 a month</small></span><span role="cell">$12,900</span></div><div role="row"><span role="rowheader">Property tax <small>City of Myrtle Beach, at the rental rate</small></span><span role="cell">$5,200</span></div><div role="row" class="tot"><span role="rowheader">Total before insurance and the loan</span><span role="cell">$18,100</span></div><p class="nt">The middle two-bedroom for sale in an oceanfront building with a Myrtle Beach address asks about $340,000. Not in the total: insurance on the unit, the loan, and the running costs of renting it.</p></div></figure><h3 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.25rem;color:var(--navy);margin:1.6rem 0 .6rem">What are HOA fees on a Myrtle Beach condo?</h3><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">The middle condo for sale charges a homeowners association (HOA) fee of about $650 a month. In oceanfront buildings the middle fee is about $925. About 1 in 5 condos for sale charge more than $1,000 a month.</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">About 8 in 10 condos for sale say the fee includes the building's insurance, which a house owner pays alone.</p></div><h3 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.25rem;color:var(--navy);margin:1.6rem 0 .6rem">What does insurance add?</h3><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">You still need a policy on your own unit, and flood cover if the building sits in a flood zone. The price depends on the unit and the cover. If you buy with us, we can get you an insurance quote for the unit before you make an offer.</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">A unit you rent out costs more to insure. In our files, a landlord policy on a house costs 15 to 20 percent more than a homeowner policy on the same house. Nationally, the Insurance Information Institute puts it at about 25 percent more.</p><h3 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.25rem;color:var(--navy);margin:1.6rem 0 .6rem">What property tax does a rental condo pay?</h3><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">A condo you rent out or keep as a second home is taxed at the 6 percent rate. The home you live in is taxed at 4 percent.</p><style>.c3-cc{margin:1.4rem 0 1.8rem;max-width:960px}.c3-cc table{width:100%;border-collapse:collapse;font-size:var(--read,1.0625rem);line-height:1.45;color:var(--muted)}.c3-cc caption{caption-side:top;text-align:left;color:var(--navy);font-weight:600;font-size:1em;line-height:1.4;padding:0 0 .7rem}.c3-cc th,.c3-cc td{padding:.6rem .7rem;text-align:left;vertical-align:top;border-bottom:1px solid var(--rule);overflow-wrap:break-word}.c3-cc thead th{color:var(--navy);font-weight:600;font-size:1em;border-bottom:2px solid var(--navy);vertical-align:bottom}.c3-cc tbody th{color:var(--navy);font-weight:600}.c3-cc .n{text-align:right;font-variant-numeric:tabular-nums}.c3-cc .i{display:inline-flex;vertical-align:-4px;margin-right:.45rem;color:var(--brass-ink)}.c3-cc tr.c3-self>*{background:#fff}@media (max-width:39.999em){.c3-cc table,.c3-cc tbody,.c3-cc tr,.c3-cc th,.c3-cc td{display:block;width:auto}.c3-cc caption{display:block}.c3-cc thead{position:absolute;width:1px;height:1px;overflow:hidden;clip:rect(0 0 0 0);white-space:nowrap}.c3-cc tbody tr{background:var(--ivory-2);border-top:3px solid var(--brass);margin:0 0 .8rem;padding:.5rem 0 .6rem}section[style*="background:var(--ivory-2)"] .c3-cc tbody tr{background:var(--ivory)}.c3-cc tr.c3-self{background:#fff!important;border:2px solid var(--brass)}.c3-cc tr.c3-self>*{background:transparent}.c3-cc th,.c3-cc td{border:0;padding:.2rem 1rem}.c3-cc tbody th{display:flex;align-items:center;font-size:1.1em;padding-bottom:.35rem}.c3-cc tbody th .i{flex:none;vertical-align:0}.c3-cc tbody th a{display:inline-block;padding:.5rem 0}.c3-cc .n{text-align:left}.c3-cc td[data-label]::before{content:attr(data-label) ": ";color:var(--navy);font-weight:500}}</style><div class="c3-cc"><table><caption>Property tax on a South Carolina condo, by how it is used</caption><thead><tr><th scope="col">How the condo is used</th><th scope="col">The tax rate applies to</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><th scope="row">The home you live in</th><td data-label="The tax rate applies to">4 percent of its value</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">A unit rented out</th><td data-label="The tax rate applies to">6 percent of its value</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">A second home</th><td data-label="The tax rate applies to">6 percent of its value</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">The rate is the share of the value that is taxed, not the tax. On the $340,000 example, 6 percent is about $20,000. The 2026 tax rates in the City of Myrtle Beach turn that into about $5,200 a year, before the exemption below. Outside city limits it would be about $4,100; some fire districts add more.</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">If you buy a condo already taxed at 6 percent and keep it at 6 percent, a quarter of its new value is exempt. The taxed value cannot fall below the county's current market value for it, so the tax inside the city could fall to about $3,900 at the lowest. You or your agent must tell the county assessor before January 31 of the first year you claim it.</p><h3 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.25rem;color:var(--navy);margin:1.6rem 0 .6rem">Who pays the lodging tax?</h3><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">The guest, on top of the price: about 13 percent in <a href="/invest/str-rules/" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline">lodging taxes</a> on a stay under 90 days inside the City of Myrtle Beach.</p></div></section><section style="background:var(--ivory-2)" id="what-it-earns"><div class="wrap"><h2 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.7rem;color:var(--navy);margin-bottom:1rem;line-height:1.3" data-toc="What it earns">What does a Myrtle Beach condo earn?</h2><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">The typical two-bedroom nightly rental in the Myrtle Beach market takes in about $20,000 a year, counting listings open only part of the year. Running costs come out of that before the HOA fee and the tax:</p><figure class="c3-gchart"><div role="table" aria-label="What a nightly rental has left each year after its running costs, against one condo's fee and tax"><span class="cap" aria-hidden="true">What a nightly rental has left each year after its running costs, against one condo's fee and tax</span><div role="rowgroup" class="sr"><div role="row"><span role="columnheader">Rental, a year</span><span role="columnheader">left a year</span></div></div><div role="rowgroup"><div role="row"><span role="rowheader"><span class="n">In the top quarter (more than 3 in 4 rentals), all year</span><span class="dot" aria-hidden="true">&middot;</span><span class="f">takes in about $44,300</span></span><span role="cell"><span class="b" style="--f:1" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="v">$27,700</span></span></div><div role="row"><span role="rowheader"><span class="n">The average rental</span><span class="dot" aria-hidden="true">&middot;</span><span class="f">takes in about $25,800</span></span><span role="cell"><span class="b" style="--f:0.577" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="v">$16,000</span></span></div><div role="row" class="ref"><span role="rowheader"><span class="n">HOA fee and tax on one typical oceanfront two-bedroom</span><span class="dot" aria-hidden="true">&middot;</span><span class="f">from the yearly cost above</span></span><span role="cell"><span class="b" style="--f:0.653" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="v">$18,100</span></span></div></div></div><figcaption>AirROI, every active Airbnb listing in the Myrtle Beach market, 12 months to September 2026. Running costs are Airbnb's fee, cleaning, utilities, supplies, repairs, a rental insurance policy and the permit, with no manager.</figcaption></figure><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Carrying the fee and tax of the typical oceanfront two-bedroom, the average rental would fall about $2,100 short. A rental in the top quarter, rented all year, would cover them and keep about $9,600 for insurance and the loan. A condo pays when it rents like the busier listings, or when its fee and tax are lower.</p><aside class="c3-cd-story" aria-label="A real owner's numbers"><p class="lb">His own numbers, not a promise</p><p>A friend of ours ran a hotel in Boston. When he retired, a house sale and his savings bought three condos in one oceanfront building here.</p><p>He had about $100,000 saved and sold his Massachusetts house for about $450,000. The three condos gross about $120,000 a year, an average of about $40,000 a condo, and roughly half of it is profit. He now helps manage fourteen units.</p></aside><h3 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.25rem;color:var(--navy);margin:1.6rem 0 .6rem">What does a rental manager take?</h3><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">It depends on how much of the work you keep. These are the ranges we see on the Grand Strand:</p><style>.c3-cc{margin:1.4rem 0 1.8rem;max-width:960px}.c3-cc table{width:100%;border-collapse:collapse;font-size:var(--read,1.0625rem);line-height:1.45;color:var(--muted)}.c3-cc caption{caption-side:top;text-align:left;color:var(--navy);font-weight:600;font-size:1em;line-height:1.4;padding:0 0 .7rem}.c3-cc th,.c3-cc td{padding:.6rem .7rem;text-align:left;vertical-align:top;border-bottom:1px solid var(--rule);overflow-wrap:break-word}.c3-cc thead th{color:var(--navy);font-weight:600;font-size:1em;border-bottom:2px solid var(--navy);vertical-align:bottom}.c3-cc tbody th{color:var(--navy);font-weight:600}.c3-cc .n{text-align:right;font-variant-numeric:tabular-nums}.c3-cc .i{display:inline-flex;vertical-align:-4px;margin-right:.45rem;color:var(--brass-ink)}.c3-cc tr.c3-self>*{background:#fff}@media (max-width:39.999em){.c3-cc table,.c3-cc tbody,.c3-cc tr,.c3-cc th,.c3-cc td{display:block;width:auto}.c3-cc caption{display:block}.c3-cc thead{position:absolute;width:1px;height:1px;overflow:hidden;clip:rect(0 0 0 0);white-space:nowrap}.c3-cc tbody tr{background:var(--ivory-2);border-top:3px solid var(--brass);margin:0 0 .8rem;padding:.5rem 0 .6rem}section[style*="background:var(--ivory-2)"] .c3-cc tbody tr{background:var(--ivory)}.c3-cc tr.c3-self{background:#fff!important;border:2px solid var(--brass)}.c3-cc tr.c3-self>*{background:transparent}.c3-cc th,.c3-cc td{border:0;padding:.2rem 1rem}.c3-cc tbody th{display:flex;align-items:center;font-size:1.1em;padding-bottom:.35rem}.c3-cc tbody th .i{flex:none;vertical-align:0}.c3-cc tbody th a{display:inline-block;padding:.5rem 0}.c3-cc .n{text-align:left}.c3-cc td[data-label]::before{content:attr(data-label) ": ";color:var(--navy);font-weight:500}}</style><div class="c3-cc"><table><caption>What a rental manager takes on the Grand Strand</caption><thead><tr><th scope="col">Who runs it</th><th scope="col">Takes, of gross profit</th><th scope="col">What it does</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><th scope="row">Hands-off management</th><td data-label="Takes, of gross profit">About 40 to 60 percent</td><td data-label="What it does">Marketing, guests, accounting, repairs</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">A basic plan</th><td data-label="Takes, of gross profit">About 13 to 17 percent</td><td data-label="What it does">Marketing, guests, basic accounting</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">You run it yourself</th><td data-label="Takes, of gross profit">Nothing</td><td data-label="What it does">You do the work</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Rental programs here are usually in oceanfront buildings run for short-term rentals. A building's own <a href="/invest/rental-program-vs-airbnb/" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline">rental program</a> is one choice; your own listing is the other.</p><div style="border-left:3px solid var(--brass);background:var(--ivory);padding:1.2rem 1.4rem;margin:1.8rem 0;max-width:720px"><p style="color:var(--navy);font-weight:600;margin-bottom:.4rem">Want the numbers on one condo before you offer?</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.7;margin-bottom:.8rem">Tell us the building or your price range. If you buy with us, an agent reads the building's rental rules, gets you an insurance quote for the unit and confirms the loan with the lender before you make an offer.</p><a class="btn btn-brass" href="/contact/">Have us check a condo</a></div></div></section><section style="background:var(--ivory)" id="renting-it"><div class="wrap"><h2 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.7rem;color:var(--navy);margin-bottom:1rem;line-height:1.3" data-toc="Renting it">Can you rent out a Myrtle Beach condo by the night?</h2><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">In most oceanfront buildings, yes. About 94 in 100 condos for sale in oceanfront buildings say they allow short-term rentals. Across all condos for sale in the county it is about 7 in 10.</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Inside the City of Myrtle Beach, nightly rentals are legal only in the zones the city shades on its <a href="/submarkets/myrtle-beach/" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline">rental map</a>, apart from 24 grandfathered houses. They are most of the oceanfront hotel and condo strip, the Broadway at the Beach area, stretches of the US 17 Bypass and US 501, and The Market Common's town center. About 3 in 4 condos in the city sit in those zones.</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Some buildings ban renting at all.</p><div style="border-left:3px solid var(--brass);background:var(--ivory-2);padding:1.2rem 1.4rem;margin:1.8rem 0;max-width:720px"><p style="color:var(--navy);font-weight:600;margin-bottom:.4rem">Want a building that lets you rent by the night?</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.7;margin-bottom:.8rem">We take buildings that ban nightly rentals out of your search. Then we read the HOA papers for any rule that would stop your plan.</p><a class="btn btn-brass" href="/contact/">Have us find rentable buildings</a></div><h3 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.25rem;color:var(--navy);margin:1.6rem 0 .6rem">Who are your neighbors in a condo building?</h3><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Mostly investors and second-home owners. About 1 in 5 condo units here is taxed at the 4 percent rate for a home its owner lives in. Of the county's other homes, about 6 in 10 are.</p><figure class="c3-gchart"><div role="table" aria-label="Homes in Horry County taxed at the 4 percent rate for a home its owner lives in"><span class="cap" aria-hidden="true">Homes in Horry County taxed at the 4 percent rate for a home its owner lives in</span><div role="rowgroup" class="sr"><div role="row"><span role="columnheader">Kind of home</span><span role="columnheader">share</span></div></div><div role="rowgroup"><div role="row"><span role="rowheader"><span class="n">Condo units</span></span><span role="cell"><span class="b" style="--f:0.215" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="v">About 1 in 5</span></span></div><div role="row"><span role="rowheader"><span class="n">Every other kind of home</span></span><span role="cell"><span class="b" style="--f:0.622" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="v">About 6 in 10</span></span></div></div></div><figcaption>County parcel records. The rest are taxed at 6 percent: rentals, second homes and homes whose owner has not claimed the lower rate.</figcaption></figure><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Most of your neighbors will rent their units out or use them part of the year.</p></div></section><section style="background:var(--ivory-2)" id="loans"><div class="wrap"><h2 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.7rem;color:var(--navy);margin-bottom:1rem;line-height:1.3" data-toc="Loans">Can you get a mortgage on a Myrtle Beach oceanfront condo?</h2><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Often not a normal one. Fannie Mae, one of the two big companies that buy normal home loans from lenders, will not buy a loan on a unit in a building <a href="/invest/condotel-financing/" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline">run like a hotel</a>, even if each unit is owned separately. The signs it lists:</p><ul class="c3-cd-list c3-cd-cols"><li>registration services that check guests in, as a hotel does</li><li>the HOA or its manager renting units by the night or for short stays</li><li>daily cleaning</li><li>one key system for every unit, run by the building</li><li>owners required to put their units in a shared rental pool</li><li>rent shared with the HOA or a rental company</li></ul><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">It also will not buy one where one person or company owns too many of the units, or in a building facing large repairs that no one has paid for.</p><h3 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.25rem;color:var(--navy);margin:1.6rem 0 .6rem">What is a non-warrantable condo?</h3><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">A condo in a building that fails these tests. Lenders call it <a href="/invest/non-warrantable-condos/" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline">non-warrantable</a>. Buildings with a rental program usually are, because so many owners are investors. That also shows the building is run for renting.</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">For you it means a larger down payment, so more of your cash stays in that unit and cannot fund the next purchase. A loan officer at our preferred lender can tell you which loans work, including a <a href="/invest/strategies/dscr-loans/" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline">DSCR loan</a>, which is approved on what the unit rents for.</p><h3 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.25rem;color:var(--navy);margin:1.6rem 0 .6rem">Can you use an FHA or VA loan on a Myrtle Beach condo?</h3><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Rarely. About 1 in 9 condos for sale say they accept an FHA loan, and about 1 in 12 a VA loan. That is what the seller's agent wrote. The FHA's own list holds only 9 approved condo buildings in Horry County, so we confirm the building with the lender before you offer.</p></div></section><section style="background:var(--ivory)" id="bargains"><div class="wrap"><h2 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.7rem;color:var(--navy);margin-bottom:1rem;line-height:1.3" data-toc="Bargains">Where are the bargains on Myrtle Beach condos?</h2><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">With an owner in trouble, in a sound building. In our own files, condos bought from a distressed owner in a stable building sold for about 22 percent less on average. The building still qualifies for normal loans; only that one unit was mismanaged.</p><div style="border-left:3px solid var(--brass);background:var(--ivory-2);padding:1.2rem 1.4rem;margin:1.8rem 0;max-width:720px"><p style="color:var(--navy);font-weight:600;margin-bottom:.4rem">Want a condo like that?</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.7;margin-bottom:.8rem">Tell us your price range. An agent looks for condos sold by an owner in trouble, in buildings that still qualify for normal loans.</p><a class="btn btn-brass" href="/contact/">Have us find you your next investment</a></div></div></section><section style="background:var(--ivory-2)" id="the-building"><div class="wrap"><h2 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.7rem;color:var(--navy);margin-bottom:1rem;line-height:1.3" data-toc="The building">What should you look for in a Myrtle Beach condo building?</h2><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">The building matters more than the unit. Two units with the same view can cost and earn different amounts because their buildings differ. Five parts of a building matter most:</p><ol class="c3-cd-steps"><li><span class="i" aria-hidden="true"><svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 24 24" width="22" height="22" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.75" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"><path d="M15 9.4c-.4-1-1.5-1.8-3-1.8-1.8 0-3 1-3 2.2 0 3 6 1.6 6 4.4 0 1.3-1.3 2.2-3 2.2-1.6 0-2.7-.7-3.1-1.8M12 5.8v1.8M12 16.4v1.8"/><circle cx="12" cy="12" r="9"/></svg></span><span><strong>The budget and the fee.</strong> What the fee pays for, how much it has risen, and whether the HOA collects enough to pay its bills.</span></li><li><span class="i" aria-hidden="true"><svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 24 24" width="22" height="22" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.75" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"><path d="M4 5h16a1 1 0 0 1 1 1v14a1 1 0 0 1-1 1H4a1 1 0 0 1-1-1V6a1 1 0 0 1 1-1zM3 10h18M8 3v4M16 3v4M8 14h.01M12 14h.01M16 14h.01M8 17.5h.01M12 17.5h.01"/></svg></span><span><strong>The savings for repairs.</strong> How much the HOA has <a href="/hoa/reserves/" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline">saved</a> for roofs, elevators and balconies, and any <a href="/hoa/special-assessments/" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline">special assessment</a> voted or planned. An older building may charge one for a large repair.</span></li><li><span class="i" aria-hidden="true"><svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 24 24" width="22" height="22" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.75" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"><path d="M10.8 12.2 20 3M16.8 6.2l2.5 2.5M14.3 8.7l2 2"/><circle cx="7.5" cy="15.5" r="4.5"/></svg></span><span><strong>The rental rules.</strong> Whether the rules allow short-term rentals, and whether the building runs its own rental program.</span></li><li><span class="i" aria-hidden="true"><svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 24 24" width="22" height="22" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.75" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"><path d="M3 11.5 12 3.5l9 8M5.5 10v10.5h13V10M10 20.5V15h4v5.5"/></svg></span><span><strong>The loan.</strong> Whether a normal loan, an FHA loan or a VA loan works in the building.</span></li><li><span class="i" aria-hidden="true"><svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 24 24" width="22" height="22" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.75" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"><path d="M4 11.5 12 4.5l8 7M6.5 10v4.5M17.5 10v4.5M2 17c1.7 1.2 3.3 1.2 5 0s3.3-1.2 5 0 3.3 1.2 5 0 3.3-1.2 5 0M2 20.5c1.7 1.2 3.3 1.2 5 0s3.3-1.2 5 0 3.3 1.2 5 0 3.3-1.2 5 0"/></svg></span><span><strong>The flood zone.</strong> About 1 in 6 condos sold last year sat in a high-risk flood zone, against about 1 in 10 other homes.</span></li></ol><h3 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.25rem;color:var(--navy);margin:1.6rem 0 .6rem">Can you owe the seller's HOA dues?</h3><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Yes, up to a limit. Under South Carolina's condo law, a buyer owes the seller's unpaid HOA dues together with the seller, unless the buyer bought at a foreclosure sale. Your share is capped at the amount on a statement the HOA must give you when you ask for it, so the amount is known before closing.</p></div></section><section style="background:var(--ivory)" id="myrtle-beach-condo-investing-faq"><div class="wrap"><p style="font-family:var(--sans);font-size:.7rem;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--brass);margin-bottom:.5rem;font-weight:600">Common questions</p><h2 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.7rem;color:var(--navy);margin-bottom:1rem;line-height:1.3">Myrtle Beach condo investing FAQ</h2><h3 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.25rem;color:var(--navy);margin:1.6rem 0 .6rem">Can you live in a Myrtle Beach condo and rent it out part of the year?</h3><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Yes, but the home you live in loses the 4 percent tax rate if it is rented out more than 72 days in a calendar year, unless its owner is on active military duty under orders. Then it is taxed at 6 percent, like any rental.</p><h3 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.25rem;color:var(--navy);margin:1.6rem 0 .6rem">What do you pay the HOA when you buy a Myrtle Beach condo?</h3><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Usually a transfer fee, and often a few months of dues paid ahead into escrow. Both are common in condo buildings here, with or without a rental program.</p><h3 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.25rem;color:var(--navy);margin:1.6rem 0 .6rem">Is it harder to sell a Myrtle Beach condo than a house?</h3><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Usually. At last year's pace of sales, the condos for sale now would take about a year to sell, and the houses and townhouses about 4 months. A condo owner should plan on more time to sell.</p><h3 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.25rem;color:var(--navy);margin:1.6rem 0 .6rem">Are most Myrtle Beach condos in big buildings?</h3><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Almost half of the county's condo units are in complexes of 100 units or more. Many others are in small buildings: the middle complex has 8 units.</p><p style="font-size:.78rem;color:var(--muted);line-height:1.7;max-width:760px;margin-top:1.6rem"><strong style="color:var(--navy)">Sources:</strong> <a href="https://www.zillow.com/research/data/" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Zillow Research</a>, <a href="https://www.airroi.com/airbnb-data/united-states/south-carolina/myrtle-beach" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">AirROI Myrtle Beach</a>, <a href="https://acclaimweb.horrycounty.org/AcclaimWeb/search/SearchTypeDocType" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Horry County deed index</a>, <a href="https://www.horrycounty.org/parcelapp/rest/services/HorryCountyGISApp/MapServer/24" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Horry County GIS</a>, <a href="https://www.scstatehouse.gov/code/t12c037.php" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">SC Code 12-37</a>, <a href="https://www.scstatehouse.gov/code/t12c043.php" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">SC Code 12-43</a>, <a href="https://www.scstatehouse.gov/code/t27c031.php" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">SC Code 27-31</a>, <a href="https://www.horrycountysc.gov/media/kufln4qp/tax-levy-2026_2.pdf" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Horry County 2026 tax levy</a>, <a href="https://www.cityofmyrtlebeach.com/departments/planning_and_zoning_department.php" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">City of Myrtle Beach zoning</a>, <a href="https://selling-guide.fanniemae.com/sel/b4-2.1-03/ineligible-projects" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Fannie Mae B4-2.1-03</a>, <a href="https://entp.hud.gov/idapp/html/condlook.cfm" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">HUD condo list</a>, <a href="https://www.iii.org/article/coverage-for-renting-out-your-home" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Insurance Information Institute</a>. Sales: county deeds; rentals: AirROI; both 12 months to September 2026. Homes for sale: CCMLS listings on our site, counted in total. Flood: FEMA zones at each sale. Not legal, tax or loan advice.</p><div class="c3ph-credits"><div class="c3ph-h" id="photo-credits">Photo credits</div><div role="list" aria-labelledby="photo-credits"><div role="listitem">A wide white condo tower and three smaller condo buildings on the dunes in North Myrtle Beach, with a road and beach houses behind them and the beach in front. Photo: U.S. Geological Survey, <a href="https://www.usa.gov/publicdomain/label/1.0/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Public domain</a>, via <a href="https://doi.org/10.5066/F7N877XX" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">U.S. Geological Survey</a>.</div><div role="listitem">Three tall oceanfront condo towers behind the dunes in Myrtle Beach, with smaller condo buildings across a curving road to the right and the beach and ocean in front. Photo: U.S. Geological Survey, <a href="https://www.usa.gov/publicdomain/label/1.0/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Public domain</a>, via <a href="https://doi.org/10.5066/F7N877XX" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">U.S. Geological Survey</a>.</div><div role="listitem">A tall tan condo tower and several low-rise condo buildings with pools on the beach at Garden City, with beach houses and a wide salt marsh behind them. Photo: U.S. Geological Survey, <a href="https://www.usa.gov/publicdomain/label/1.0/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Public domain</a>, vi